t which is saved in the numb items  variable and then I have the add item  method which acts on the num items  variable and I return the function and  the variable from the setup function  anything I returned from the setup  function can be used normally in my  template and in the rest of my component  so this allows me to organize my code by  feature because I can put my data  anywhere I want I don't have to just  have it in one data object I can put my  methods anywhere I want not just some  one methods object etc  so you can look at those examples on  jsfiddle if you want  so of course you don't want to keep all  of this inside one component you that's  why there's basically what they call a  composition functions so you can take  all of these low-level api's and import  them into any JavaScript file from view  make a function like here I have used  cart function and use search function if  if you come from react then you might  recognize this syntax with hooks it's  kind of similar how it works here so  basically you have the used cart and use  search functions and you can use them  inside your setup function you pass any  kind of reactive elements into them or  you can just use those reactive elements  from the file where the function is  created and then you return the bindings  from those functions and they are  explicitly named so you know where  something is coming from in your  component there's no confusion like  there used to be with some former  solutions like mix-ins inside of you and  there's no namespace conflicts unless  you create it yourself so in review of  what composition is there composable  functions that you use inside of a setup  method it gives you logical organization  by feature there is much better code  reuse so if you've used view in the past  you probably know you're writing the  same thing in multiple components at  least when you're creating production  level applications so now you can  abstract away the logic for the feature  and